% c262 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 469-470 sec. 7: V9 belongs in root position; PDF 463-466, 479 point 4): 192 bass E flat2 (root; the V6/5 dated from cycle 12, when 187-198 were in C and a root V-I had to be avoided; since v74 they are in A flat), so the cellos' G3 no longer doubles a leading-tone bass; Bn 1 passes G3-F3-E flat3 (9th) and the cellos take the 7th D flat3 to C3 at 193 (it rose to E flat3), as at 72-73.
\version "2.24.0"
% c126: Cl 2 and Bsn 1 breathe after 178 with the solo's breath (pp pads 171-182).
% SECOND THEME IN THE RECAPITULATION, bars 171-198. 171-186: theme F2 in C minor, muted trumpet 1 in unison with the cor anglais
% (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 1: on a repeat, a muted trumpet doubling the cor anglais); oboe 1 doubles the climax in unison and flute 1 at the octave (181-183, c011, as flute 1 at 61-63).
% Accompaniment as at 51. Deceptive cadence at 186 (V - VI): the C-major statement starts from A flat.
% 187-198: theme F2 in C major in the violins with the cellos' counter-melody (as at 67, a minor third lower); its consequent turns
% to E flat over A flat (mixture, 198), and the coda's C-minor tutti follows. Movement I's withheld sounds stay for the coda.
% c073 (form; A&S PDF 95, Ex. 6-4: a tonic's meaning depends on its function; the C-major tonic belongs to the arrival at 223 and the cadence at 263):
% 187-197 are transposed into A flat major (\transpose c aes,), the key that the deceptive cadence at 186 opens; 198 (A flat with E flat on top) is unchanged and is now
% the statement's own tonic, reached plagally (197 D flat - 198 A flat), ff into the C-minor tutti (VI - i, as before). C major, and the violas' E natural
% (they began 187 on E3, the thread pitch class), are now first heard at 219-223. Cb 190, 193-194 and violas 194 are re-voiced inside the range (Cb E1, viola C3).

% c103 (form; A&S Unit 26, PDF 448 Ex. 26-7, PDF 456 Point 4; corpus Dvorak 9/iv cadence.csv): 183 bass E flat (i6; was A flat, VI). In the exposition the cor anglais
% theme has a deceptive cadence at 63 and an authentic one at 66; the recapitulation must avoid root V-i in C, so 186 became deceptive too, and 182-183 / 185-186 were two
% identical G-A flat deceptive cadences three bars apart (with 165-166 and 204-205, four in 40 bars, all onto A flat). 183 now closes on the inverted tonic (V-i6, allowed),
% so the deceptive cadence at 186 is heard as the turn into A flat. Violas G3-C4 at 183 (were E flat3-C4: octaves G-E flat with the cellos).
% c032 (harmony; A&S PDF 283): 177 Cl 2 C4 on beat 3 (was F3: F-G under the solo's C-D, parallel 5ths into V at 178).
% c041 (Adler ch. 7, PDF 185-186, 201, 205): 181-183 Fl 1 at the unison (was G6, the shrill register), Ob 1 rests (a unison oboe overshadowed the cor anglais, whose G5 = written D6 is its thin, pinched register); the climax keeps cor anglais + muted trumpet with flute colour.
% c074 (orchestration; Adler ch. 5, PDF 135: background away from the register of a melody of similar colour): 171-180 Cl 1 rests; its C4/B3 line doubled Bn 1 an octave higher
% and met the cor anglais and muted trumpet at the unison on C4 (172, 177, 180). It re-enters at 181 under the solo's high bars.
% c074 (Adler ch. 5, PDF 127, 141, 151-152): 187-196 Vln II play the tune in unison with Vln I (they were an octave lower, down to A flat3-B flat3 on the G string, their weakest register);
% the violas, with Hn 1, take the lower octave (E flat4-A flat3, their middle strings) instead of doubling the cellos' counter-melody on the C string; the cellos carry the counter alone; violas rejoin it at 197.
% c058 (expression; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 12-13 economy): 195-198 clarinets rest as at 75-78 (c047): they doubled Hn 1-2 at the unison, pp under the horns' p.
% c148 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 53-54: 195-198 is a four-bar group whose goal is the next strong bar, 199; PDF 50: a held note's continuation is weaker than its attack): 198 the held chord (Fl 1, Ob 1, Bn, Hn 1-2, strings) crescendos f < into the ff at 199, as 82 does into 83; c068's 'full crescendo 196-198' had stopped at 198's downbeat. Bowing and the down-bow at 198 unchanged.
% c345 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues during long periods of rest, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part; score and MIDI unchanged.
recSFluteOne = { R1*8 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "tptOne" #DOWN { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Tpt. 1" } } | r2 r4 \once \override Staff.Hairpin.springs-and-rods = #ly:spanner::set-spacing-rods \once \override Staff.Hairpin.minimum-length = #7 d''4(\p\< | g''4\mf f''4\> es''4 d''4) | c''2.(\p bes'4) | R1*3 | R1*10 | \transpose c aes, { c'''2.(\p\< d'''4 } | es'''1)\f\< | }
% c165 (MOLA PDF 5; as c105/c135): Ob 1 rests 167-196 (30 bars) and enters p in the poco rit. at 197: Vln I cue at 195-196. The contrabassoon rests 167-198 and
% enters ff at 199 like the low brass that c105 cued: the cellos at 197-198 (\transposedCueDuring c, so its octave-up part shows them at sounding pitch). Score and MIDI unchanged.
% c224 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, adler2 PDF 178: the colour of each theme combines at least one string, one woodwind and one brass instrument; PDF 179: the big sound at p needs mixed-colour doubling; PDF 170-171, Mahler 4/iv: a recurring passage is coloured differently each time; PDF 176, Jupiter: Vln II doubled by Ob 1 at pitch): 187-194 Ob 1 doubles F2 in A flat at pitch with Vln I-II, p dolce, one level under the violins' mf, breath after 190 with Hn 1; rests 195-196 and re-enters at 197 as before. F2's notes unchanged.
% c337 (texture; Adler ch. 16, adler2 PDF 213 and Ex. XVI-10 m. 25-32, PDF 211: in Dvorak's answer to the strain 'the delay of the oboe entrance clarifies the tune much better, and makes the last four measures sound truly polyphonic'; PDF 229: two like colours doubled at pitch swallow each other up; PDF 228: a solo entrance is made fresh by what precedes it): 191-193 Ob 1 no longer doubles F2 at pitch with Vln I-II; it re-enters in 194, where the tune holds B flat4 for a bar, and echoes the tune's 192 at pitch (E flat5-D flat5-C5-B flat4, first two notes as eighths after a quarter rest, so it does not sound E flat5 against Cl 1's F flat4 on 194.1.5). 187-190 doubling (c224), the tune, the anticipation at 193.4 in Vln I-II, Vla and Hn 1, and 195-198 unchanged.
% c339 (Rimsky-Korsakov, Principles, PDF 128-129: 'A wood-wind instrument cannot be used to echo the strings ... on account of the dissimilarity in timbre'; 'imitation in octaves (with a decrease in resonance) creates an effect resembling an echo'): 194 Ob 1 no longer echoes the violins' bar 192 at pitch (c337); the cellos and Bn 1 already answer its tail D flat-C-B flat two octaves lower in that bar. Ob 1 rests 191-196 as in v329.
recSOboeOne = { R1*16 | \transpose c aes, { g''2.(\p^\markup \column { "1." \italic "dolce" } e''4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''4(\< c''4 e''4 g''4) } | \transpose c aes, { d'''2(\> c'''4 b''4) } | \transpose c aes, { a''2(\! g''2) \breathe } | R1*4 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | \transpose c aes, { c''2.(\p\< d''4 } | es''1)\f\< | }
recSCorAnglais = { g'2.(\p^\markup \italic "dolce cantabile" es'4 | d'4 c'4 es'4 g'4 | d''2 c''4 bes'4 | aes'2 g'2) \breathe | f'2.( aes'4 | g'4 f'4 es'4 d'4 | es'2 c'2 | d'1) \breathe | g'2.( es'4 | d'4 c'4 es'4 g'4\< | c''2. d''4 | g''4\mf f''4\> es''4 d''4) \breathe | c''2.(\p bes'4 | aes'4 g'4 f'4 es'4 | d'2 b2 | c'1) | R1*12 | }
% c207 (texture; Adler ch. 5, PDF 132: foreground, middleground and background; PDF 135, Ex. V-7 Brahms 3/iii: a soft, repetitive background figure "adds intensity to the
% longer notes of the melody", dovetailed "without any pauses in the texture", in a pale register and a rhythm radically unlike the tune's; PDF 163, Ex. V-39: one part holds the
% figure's low note so the broken chord sustains like a pedal; PDF 136-137, Ex. V-13: the figure stops so the next phrase sounds changed): 187-194 Cl 1-2 pp in the chalumeau
% (A flat3-F4), Cl 2 holding the chord's low tone, Cl 1 rocking eighths after an eighth rest; the only cantabile major statement of the movement that had no background
% (67 and 123 have the harp, 235 harp and horns; the harp stays silent 187-198 as c063 decided, so its 235 entry is still fresh). The figure stops at 195, where the head
% returns with the horns, oboe and flute (crescendo by addition kept). Inner voices checked against the bass for octaves and fifths (190 beats 3-4 E flat4, not C4, so no D flat-C octaves with the bass).
% Cl 1's pp is part-only: on the shared score staff Cl 2's pp on the downbeat stands for both (equal dynamics).
% c210 (cyclic; A&S Unit 32, PDF 600-601 sec. 16: the Appassionata's turn to A flat permits the basic 5-flat6-5 motive transposed as E flat-F flat-E flat, later reinterpreted
% enharmonically as E flat-E natural-E flat; PDF 597-598, Ex. 32-16/17: the remote area is tied to the home key by one pitch that changes spelling, E flat/D sharp; PDF 603, review 5:
% relate large-scale chromaticism to the motivic design): 194 beat 1, Cl 1's figure takes F flat4 as the minor ninth over V7 of A flat (E flat bass, cellos D flat3, tune B flat4
% held) and resolves it at once to E flat4 (was B flat3-E flat4-B flat3). The recapitulation's A flat area (flat VI) now carries F1's 5-flat6-5 in its own key, at the
% pitch of movement II's V minor-ninth (II 80-81, Hn 3 F flat4 to E flat4) and of movement I's A flat-minor colour (I 76); F flat4 is the thread pitch E4 in flat spelling, which
% falls back to E flat here and rises as D sharp4-E4 only at 219-220. The movement's only F flat; it resolves to E flat (cyclic rule). Cl 2 G3, strings and bassoons unchanged.
% c305 (Gould, Behind Bars ch. 2, PDF 66: keep noteheads separate; a semibreve must not appear joined to a stem; editions reverse the order so the semibreve is never beside a stem): score-only force-hshift at 185 (Cl 1-2) puts the semibreve on the stem-free side of the other part's note, 0.15-0.2 space apart
recSClOne = { R1*10 | c'1\pp | c'2 b2 | c'1 | c'2 b2 | \tag #'score { \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.1 } g1 | c'1 | \partCombineApart r8 aes8(-\tag #'part \pp c'8 aes8) r8 aes8( c'8 aes8) | r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) r8 aes8( c'8 aes8) | r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) | r8 des'8( f'8 des'8) r8 aes8( es'8 aes8) | r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) | r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) r8 bes8( des'8 bes8) | r8 aes8( c'8 aes8) r8 aes8( f8 aes8) | r8 fes'8( es'8 bes8) r8 bes8( es'8 bes8) \partCombineAutomatic | R1*4 | }
% c216 (as sOne.ily 51; Adler PDF 211, Ex. VII-57): 171 Cl 2 restarts its pp pad dal niente under the entry of the cor anglais and muted trumpet.
% c216 (Adler PDF 175, 188): 186 Cl 2 breathes at the double bar before M (183-194 is otherwise 12 bars pp without a rest; it restrikes at 187 in any case).
% c263 (A&S Unit 15, PDF 243 and 245 point 9: the diminished triad on the leading tone is seldom satisfactory in 5/3 position; V6/5, which contains its three tones, is preferred): 172 and 180 Cl 2 G3 (was F3) on beats 1-2, so the neighbour chord over the bass B is V6/5 (B-D-F-G), as the exposition's 52 and 60 are V6/5 since c093; the seventh F stays in the violas (F3 -> E flat3).
% c361 (A&S Unit 20, PDF 341 sec. 16 and PDF 342 sec. 17, review point 11 (PDF 343): 'No passing or neighboring tone can break up octaves or unisons'): Cl 2's held F3 (191) fell to E flat3 on 192.1 in octaves with the tune's F -> E flat (violas and Hn 1 F4 -> E flat4, violins F5 -> E flat5); c361's accented passing tone on 192.1 only displaces them by half a beat. Cl 2 now takes G3 at 192 (the third of V7, rising from F3 against the falling tune) and returns to E flat3 on 193.1 (was E flat3 tied 192-193.2).
recSClTwo = { es1\pp^\markup \italic "dal niente" | g2 es2 | f1 | f2 es2 | f1 | g2 f2 | es2 c'2 | g4( f4 d4 f4) \breathe | g2 es2 | g2 es2 | es2 aes2~ | aes2 g4 r4 | es1 | f1 | \tag #'score { \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 } g2 f2 | es1 \breathe | es1\pp~ | es1 | f1 | aes2 es2 | f1 | g1 | es2 f2 | g1 | R1*4 | }
% c035 (Gould PDF 65): 195 and 197 Bn 1 and Bn 2 exchange notes (C/E, C-C/E-F) so Bn 1 stays the upper part; same sound.
% c092 (A&S PDF 83, Ex. 5-18: parallel octaves; PDF 76: the leading tone not doubled): 185-186 Bn 1 B2-D3 | C3 (was B2-G2 | A flat2) and Cl 1 G3 | C4 (was G3 | A flat3): both moved G-A flat in octaves with the bass into the deceptive cadence; VI now doubles its 3rd C.
% c205 (Gould PDF 50-51): 190.4 and 198.4 Bn 1's crotchet rest moved one space down into the upper half of the stave (it stood across the top line above two low parts). 194.4 stays: Bn 2's E flat3 crosses above Bn 1 there.
% c217 (texture; Adler ch. 15, PDF 176: in the Jupiter coda the cellos are doubled by the first bassoon at pitch and each rendition of a theme is coloured differently; PDF 178-179: each element in a mixed colour of its own): 187-194 Bn 1 doubles the cellos' counter-melody to F2 at pitch, p (it held A flat 2-G2-F2, the bass an octave above the basses, already in Bn 2). The tune is strings + horn, the counter now strings + bassoon; the exposition's 67-76 keeps the cellos alone.
% c312 (A&S Unit 22, PDF 375 sec. 4, Ex. 22-3c: a diminished 5th between upper voices that moves to a perfect 5th in similar motion makes obtrusive hidden fifths, "avoid"):
%   178 Bn 1 B2-D3 (was B2 held): its B2 under Cl 2's F3 (d5) went to C3 under G3 (P5) at 179. Now the exposition's layout at 58 (Bn 1 D3-F3), transposed: D3-C3 against F3-G3 in contrary motion.
recSBsnOne = { es2\pp c2 | b,2 c2 | c1 | c1 | c1 | b,1 | c1 | b,2 d2 \breathe | c1 | b,2 c2 | c1 | c2 g,4 r4 | c1 | c2 b,2 | b,2 d2 | c1 | \transpose c aes, { e2(\p f4 g4 } | \transpose c aes, { f2 e2) } | \transpose c aes, { f4( g4 a2 } | \transpose c aes, { c'4 d'4 e'2) \breathe } | \transpose c aes, { c'2( a4 c'4 } | \transpose c aes, { b2 a2) } | \transpose c aes, { g2( a2 } | \transpose c aes, { f4 e4 d2) } | \transpose c aes, { e1 } | \transpose c aes, { d2 c2 } | \transpose c aes, { e2\< f2 } | es2.\f\< \tag #'score { \once \override Rest.extra-offset = #'(0 . -1) } r4 | }
% c164 (Adler ch. 5, PDF 135, Ex. V-26: wider intervals between the lower parts, as in the overtone series): 194 Bn 2 E flat2 then E flat3 on beats 3-4, a ninth above the Cb's D flat2 (as Bn 2's B flat2 over A flat1 at 74), not a major second above it (c163's V4/2).
% c216 (playability; Adler ch. 6, adler1 PDF 175: consider the player's limits; PDF 226: the low bassoon takes a firm, full breath): 198 Bn 2 rests on beat 4 with Bn 1 (both breathe before the ff tutti at 199) after 12 bars of bass line without a rest; Vc and Cb carry the bass.
% c256 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37): Bn 2 breathes at the end of 190 with Bn 1 and Hn 1 (it held 187-198 without a rest, about 17 s).
% c357 (texture; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 19 Ex. 3A: avoid harmonizing a melody the same way twice, a stepwise falling bass reaching the same resting place; PDF 20: a substitution for a lift; PDF 14: the bass rises to the secondary focus level): 188 beat 3 bass G flat (Cb G flat1, Bn 2 G flat2; was A flat), so F2's second bar in A flat is V6/5 - V4/2 of IV and the bass falls A flat-G-G flat-F into 189 under the rising tune (exposition 68 and recapitulation 196 keep V6/5 - I). The cellos' C3 is the third of A flat7 and rises to D flat3 at 189; tune, counter-melody, scoring, dynamics and bowing unchanged.
recSBsnTwo = { R1*16 | \transpose c aes, { c1\p } | g,2 ges,2 | \transpose c aes, { a,1 } | \transpose c aes, { f,2 e,2 \breathe } | \transpose c aes, { d,1 } | \transpose c aes, { b,1 } | \transpose c aes, { c2 f,4 fis,4 } | \transpose c aes, { g,2 g2 } | \transpose c aes, { c1 } | \transpose c aes, { b,2 c2 } | \transpose c aes, { c2\< c2 } | aes,2.\f\< r4 | }
% c059 (holistic, Tchaikovsky 4/iv; Adler part 1 PDF 238 Schubert: each thematic element scored differently; PDF 255: the third time, alter the scoring; PDF 267 Brahms 1/ii and
% PDF 270 Copland: one wind quietly doubling a string line): 187-194 Hn 1 p dolce doubles Vln II (the tune's lower octave), so the C-major statement is no longer scored exactly as 67-74.
% c092 (A&S PDF 83, PDF 87 point 7: octaves with leaps in both voices): 197 Hn 2 E flat3-F3 (was A flat3-F3: E flat3-A flat3 moved in octaves with the tune's E flat5-A flat5).
% c166 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 44): Hn 1-2 breathe at the end of 198; Hn 1 played 191-204 (about 20 s, f < into the ff tutti at 199) without a rest.
% c297 (Adler ch. 11, adler2 PDF 50 and 52-54, Ex. XI-29 (Druckman): open and closed brass must be marked exactly in every part, 'some instruments are open and some closed'): the first horn note after a stopped passage carries 'o' (open), as retr 139 has: Hn 1 at 67 (stopped 49-50), Hn 1 at 187 and Hn 2 at 195 (stopped 168-169).
recSHnOne = { R1*16 | \transpose c aes, { g'2.(\p-\open^\markup \italic "dolce" e'4) } | \transpose c aes, { d'4(\< c'4 e'4 g'4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''2(\> c''4 b'4) } | \transpose c aes, { a'2(\! g'2) \breathe } | \transpose c aes, { f'2.( a'8 b'8) } | \transpose c aes, { a'8( g'8 f'4 e'4 d'4) } | \transpose c aes, { e'2( c'4 d'4~ } | \transpose c aes, { d'1) } | \transpose c aes, { c'1\p } | \transpose c aes, { d'2 c'2 } | \transpose c aes, { e'2\< f'2 } | es'1\f\< \breathe | }
% c345 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues during long periods of rest, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part; score and MIDI unchanged.
recSHnTwo = { R1*16 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | \transpose c aes, { g1\p-\open } | \transpose c aes, { g2 g2 } | \transpose c aes, { g2\< a4 aes4 } | es1\f\< \breathe | }  % c270: Hn 2 F3-F flat3-E flat3 with the violas, movement II's Hn 4 line at II 87-89 (open here)
% c075 (MOLA PDF 5, Example 5): the mute comes off in the rest at 187; "senza sord." stays at 199
% c349 (Rimsky-Korsakov, Principles, PDF 140-141: doubling a solo melody 'in unison or octaves ... incessant duplication for an extended period of time should be avoided;
%   it is only permissible in isolated phrases', since it 'replaces by a mixed timbre the rare characteristic qualities' of the solo; PDF 142 and Ex. 284 (PDF 457), Ex. 289 (PDF 467):
%   the cor anglais joins the voice 'colla voce' for chosen phrases only): 175-178 the muted trumpet rests, so F2's second phrase is the cor anglais alone (its own colour,
%   as in movement I's coda duet and movement II's theme B); the trumpet rejoins at 179 for the climb to the peak (Fl 1 at 181-183). CA, accompaniment and dynamics unchanged.
recSTptOne = { g'2.(\p^\markup \italic "con sord., dolce cantabile" es'4 | d'4 c'4 es'4 g'4 | d''2 c''4 bes'4 | aes'2 g'2) \breathe | R1*4 | g'2.(\p-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." es'4 | d'4 c'4 es'4 g'4\< | c''2. d''4 | g''4\mf f''4\> es''4 d''4) \breathe | c''2.(\p bes'4 | aes'4 g'4 f'4 es'4 | d'2 b2 | c'1) | R1*12^\markup \italic "togli sord." | }
% c063 (form; Adler II PDF 263-264, 267: a colour kept back stays fresh, Mozart keeps the orchestral horns and flutes silent while a soloist of that kind plays;
% PDF 261-262: roles exchanged): the harp's only texture in the movement is broken-chord eighths under a major-mode cantabile (67, 123, 187, 235); 187-198 were 67-78 again,
% harp included. The harp rests at 187-198, so its fourth use, under the owed C-major second-theme head at 235, comes after 104 bars of silence; the eighth-note motion
% moves to the minor half (violas 171-186).
recSHarpR = { R1*16 | R1*12 | }
recSHarpL = { R1*16 | R1*12 | }
% c138 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 151 and 155: a lyrical string legato swells into the note of arrival; PDF 150, 152: the expressive crescendo and diminuendo are what notation must ask for): 188-190 a swell < > into the phrase's peak at 189 in Vln I-II, violas and Hn 1, as the cor anglais's statement has at 180-182; same at 68-70 (sTwo.ily).
% c165 (as at 67; c065): no "arco" for the violins at 187. Vln I never plays pizz. in the movement, Vln II last plucked at 115-122 and has bowed since 127.
% c261 (A&S Unit 3, PDF 56 Ex. 3-19): 193.4 the tune's B flat enters a beat early, tied into 194 (Vln I, Vln II, Vla, Ob 1, Hn 1), as at 73-74.
% c301 (melody; Adler ch. 3, adler1 PDF 75, Ex. III-7 Brahms 3/iii: in soft lyrical passages the higher notes stay on the A string rather than cross to the brighter E string; PDF 73: timbre is the reason to name a string):
%   189-192 Vln I-II "sul A" on F2's peak and descent in A flat (B flat5-A flat5-G5 | F5-E flat5 | D flat5-F5 | E flat5-D flat5-C5-B flat4), so the recapitulated statement is veiled where 69-72 is on the E string, and mixes with Ob 1 at pitch. Notes, slurs, bowing and dynamics unchanged; A flat4 at 188, 193, 196 lies outside the spanner.
% c301 (Adler ch. 3, adler1 PDF 103: pizzicato cello and timpani is one of the most used couplings): 178 the pizz. cellos play the timpani's motive x with them, g,4 g,8 g,8 g,4 r4 pp (was G2 on beat 1 only), so the near-the-rim quotation of the scherzo's closing figure has a pitch; the scherzo ends the same way, its last timpani E with pizz. cellos and basses (scherzo 375). Basses keep beat 1.
% c318 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38 and Ex. II-20, Coriolanus): 198 Vln I-II down-bow then up-bow on the held E flat (was a down-bow crescendo ending at the tip, a retake in an eighth rest before 199's down-bow pickup); the f attack stays a down-bow, the crescendo is on the up-bow. Vla 198 E flat and C flat on separate bows (slur removed), C flat up-bow, for the same reason.
% c361 (melody; A&S Unit 20, PDF 337, Ex. 20-17: escape tone + accented passing tone decorating a falling second): 191.4-192.1 the violins F5-G5 | F5-E flat5 over ii7 -> V7 of A flat (violas and Hn 1 an octave lower), as at 71.4-72.1 in the E flat-major statement (sTwo.ily). G5 is a dissonant escape tone over ii7 (tritone with Cl 1's D flat4), F5 on 192.1 the ninth over V7, resolving on the off-beat. Scoring, dynamics and bowing unchanged.
recSVlnOne = { R1*16 | \transpose c aes, { g''2.(\mf^\markup \italic "cantabile" e''4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''4(\< c''4 e''4 g''4) } | \transpose c aes, { \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul A" d'''2(\>\startTextSpan c'''4 b''4) } | \transpose c aes, { a''2(\! g''2) } | \transpose c aes, { f''2.( a''8 b''8) } | \transpose c aes, { a''8( g''8 f''4 e''4 d''4)\stopTextSpan } | \transpose c aes, { e''2( c''4 d''4~ } | \transpose c aes, { d''1) } | \transpose c aes, { g''2.( e''4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''4( c''4 e''4 g''4)\< } | \transpose c aes, { c'''2. d'''4\upbow } | es'''1\f\<\dnup | }
% c063 (form, the recapitulation's second hearing; Adler ch. 17, PDF 257-258 dialogue with a changed answering colour, PDF 267 the orchestra reduced when the solo lies in its soft register,
% PDF 274 accompaniment kept registrally distant from a woodwind solo, PDF 288 Haydn changes the doubling on the repeat): 171-186 Vln II rest (their pizz. C4-F4 lay inside the cor anglais's low fifth,
% B3-F4); the solo sounds above violas, cellos, basses and the low wood-wind pad only, so the C-minor statement no longer repeats 51-66's background a third lower, and Vln II's arco entry at 187 is a fresh colour.
recSVlnTwo = { R1*16 | \transpose c aes, { g''2.(\mf^\markup \italic \override #'(baseline-skip . 2.3) \column { "cantabile" "unis. with Vln I" } e''4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''4(\< c''4 e''4 g''4) } | \transpose c aes, { \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul A" d'''2(\>\startTextSpan c'''4 b''4) } | \transpose c aes, { a''2(\! g''2) } | \transpose c aes, { f''2.( a''8 b''8) } | \transpose c aes, { a''8( g''8 f''4 e''4 d''4)\stopTextSpan } | \transpose c aes, { e''2( c''4 d''4~ } | \transpose c aes, { d''1) } | \transpose c aes, { g''2.( e''4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''4( c''4 e''4 g''4)\< } | \transpose c aes, { c''2. d''4\upbow } | es''1\f\<\dnup | }
% c063 (form; Adler II PDF 288, Haydn: on a repeat the scoring changes, "devices like these add great variety"; PDF 269-270, Sibelius VC and Bartok PC 3: a soft,
% rhythmically independent weaving background under the solo; PDF 259-260): 171-186 were 51-66 transposed in every accompanying part. The violas now weave arco sul tasto pp
% eighths on the same dyads (E flat3-A flat3, an octave and more below the cor anglais and muted trumpet), so the recapitulated second theme keeps the movement's eighth-note
% motion and is not a copy of 51; 187 'ord.' (they are already arco and unis.).
% c067 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 142 Ex. 202: sul tasto to natural sound adds body at the climax; PDF 144 Ex. 194): 181-182 the violas play ord. p < mp > under the solo's peak, sul tasto pp again from 183.
% c114 (balance; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 51: in soft passages one string section = one wind; c094/c098 set 67's counter-melody and bass mp under the mf tune): 187 violas, cellos and basses mp (were mf, level with the tune in Vln I+II unison), crescendo 196, mf 197, f 198 as before.
% c293 (form; A&S Unit 32, PDF 596 Ex. 32-15 Appassionata: the major area is inflected to minor before the music moves on; PDF 601-602 Ex. 32-21/22, Mozart K. 542: flat VI reached by a deceptive cadence is turned to flat VI minor instead of relaxing; PDF 585: a rising inflection increases intensity and draws the ear to the key change): 198 beats 3-4 violas and cellos C flat4 (were C4): the A flat statement reaches its major tonic on the downbeat (iv with F flat at 197.4 - I, as movement II 87-89) and is then inflected to A flat minor, and the cellos rise C flat4 -> C4 into the C-minor tutti at 199 under the held E flat on top (cellos C4-C flat4 in one bow).
recSVla = { es8( g es g) es8( g es g) | d8( f d f) es8( g es g) | f8( aes f aes) f8( aes f aes) | f8( aes f aes) es8( g es g) | d8( f d f) d8( f d f) | d8( g d g) d8( f d f) | es8( g es g) f8( c' f c') | d8( g d g) d8( f d f) | es8( g es g) es8( g es g) | d8( f d f) es8( g es g) | es8(\p\<^\markup \italic "ord." g es g) d8( f d f) | es8(\mp aes es aes)\> d8( g d g) | g8(\pp^\markup \italic "sul tasto" c' g c') g8( c' g c') | f8( aes f aes) d8( f d f) | d8( g d g) d8( f d f) | aes8( c' aes c') r2 | \transpose c aes, { g'2.(\mp^\markup \italic "ord., espress." e'4) } | \transpose c aes, { d'4(\< c'4 e'4 g'4) } | \transpose c aes, { d''2(\> c''4 b'4) } | \transpose c aes, { a'2(\! g'2) } | \transpose c aes, { f'2.( a'8 b'8) } | \transpose c aes, { a'8( g'8 f'4 e'4 d'4) } | \transpose c aes, { e'2( c'4 d'4~ } | \transpose c aes, { d'1) } | \transpose c aes, { g'2.( e'4) } | \transpose c aes, { d'4(\< c'4 e'4 g'4) } | \transpose c aes, { g2(\mf\< a4 aes4) } | es2\f\< ces'2\upbow | }  % c270 (A&S Unit 23, PDF 387-388 Ex. 23-2e/f, 23-9; PDF 395 pts 2a, 3): 197 beat 4 F-flat3, the iv/II6-5 with flat 6 of A flat, F3-F flat3-E flat3 as movement II's lower violas and Hn 4 at II 87-89 (same pitch); C4 returns on 198 beat 3
% c027 (texture; Adler II PDF 259-260): 189-190 counter-melody as at 69-70, F-G-A | A-B-C (no octaves with the tune's C-B-A-G). 186 Cl 1 A flat3 below the solo's C4 (was E flat4 above it; Adler II PDF 274).
% c051 (Sebesky PDF 208, Ex. 273): 194 Vc/Vla F-E-D-F under the tune's held D (as 74), the 7th resolving to E at 195.
% c097: 190-193 the cellos' counter-melody follows the change at 70-73 (sTwo.ily): C-D-E | C-A-C | B-F | G-A-G in the C template, in sixths and tenths under the tune instead of octaves.
% c121 (Adler ch. 3, PDF 99: the cello's G string is the least strong and carries less well; PDF 91 Ex. III-39: violas and cellos coupled on a counter-line):
% 187 the cellos carry the counter-melody alone (the violas left it for the tune's lower octave at c074), starting C3-E flat3 on the G string, so they play mf with
% the tune instead of mp (c114's mp assumed a counter in two sections, as at 67); they hold mf to 196 and crescendo from 197 to f at 198.
% c137 (texture; Adler ch. 16, PDF 230-231: the countertheme keeps its own line; A&S PDF 83 octaves; as c092 for Hn 2 at 197): 197 beats 3-4 violas and cellos F3 (were A flat 3-B flat 3 in octaves with the tune's A flat-B flat in Vln I-II);
% the counter-melody rises E flat-F-A flat under the tune (no octaves with the basses' D flat-A flat either) instead of joining it.
recSVc = { c4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | b,4 r4 c4 r4 | aes,4 r4 r2 | f,4 r4 es,4 r4 | d,4 r4 r2 | b,4 r4 r2 | c4 r4 aes,4 r4 | g,4 g,8 g,8 g,4 r4 | es,4 r4 r2 | b,4 r4 c4 r4 | es,4 r4 f,4 r4 | f,4 r4 g,4 r4 | es,4 r4 r2 | f,4 r4 g,4 r4 | g,4 r4 r2 | aes,4 r4 r2 | \transpose c aes, { e2(\mf^\markup \italic "arco, espress." f4 g4) } | \transpose c aes, { f2( e2) } | \transpose c aes, { f4( g4 a2) } | \transpose c aes, { c'4( d'4 e'2) } | \transpose c aes, { c'2( a4 c'4) } | \transpose c aes, { b2( f2) } | \transpose c aes, { e2( a2) } | \transpose c aes, { f4( e4 d2) } | \transpose c aes, { e2( f4 g4) } | \transpose c aes, { f2( e2) } | \transpose c aes, { g2(\< a4 c'4) } | c'2\f\<( ces'2) | }  % c270: 197 beat 4 A flat3 (was F3 held), so the violas' and Hn 2's F flat3 is not doubled against an F natural; C4 at 198 unchanged
% c246 (playability; Adler App. A, adler2 PDF 302: the bass's professional range reaches C1 with the C extension, marked 'not found on every instrument'; adler1 PDF 108: two or three basses in most orchestras have it, it exists so that doubled cello passages stay an octave below without switching octaves, and players without it take the octave above automatically): 174.3, 175, 179, 181 and 183 the pizz. basses take E flat1 and D1 an octave below the cellos, as in the exposition (51-66), instead of folding up to the cellos' unison.
recSCb = { c,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | b,,4 r4 c,4 r4 | aes,,4 r4 r2 | f,,4 r4 es,,4 r4 | d,,4 r4 r2 | b,,4 r4 r2 | c,4 r4 aes,,4 r4 | g,,4 r4 r2 | es,,4 r4 r2 | b,,4 r4 c,4 r4 | es,,4 r4 f,,4 r4 | f,,4 r4 g,,4 r4 | es,,4 r4 r2 | f,,4 r4 g,,4 r4 | g,,4 r4 r2 | aes,,4 r4 r2 | \transpose c aes, { c,1\mp^\markup \italic "arco" } | g,,2 ges,,2 | \transpose c aes, { a,,1 } | des,2 c,2 | \transpose c aes, { d,1 } | es,1 | aes,,2 des,4 d,4 | es,2 des,2 | \transpose c aes, { e,1 } | \transpose c aes, { b,,2\< c,2 } | \transpose c aes, { e,2\mf\< f,2 } | aes,,1\f\< | }

% c105 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues are expected during long rests, audible and obvious, in the reading key): part-only cues (tag 'part) in 197-198 for the
% brass and timpani that enter ff/f at N (199) after 32 bars of rest (31 for the timpani) through L, M and a poco rit.: Vln I's climb to E flat 6 f for the
% trumpets and horns (horns an octave lower, \transposedCueDuring, so the cue stays on the stave), the cellos' line for the trombones, tuba and timpani.
% c135 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues during long periods of rest; Example 4, PDF 10): Ob 2 rests 167-198 (32 bars, through the Poco meno mosso, M and the poco rit.) and enters ff at 199 with no cue,
% while the brass with 28 bars of rest have one (c105). Part-only cue of Ob 1, its stand partner, at 197-198. Score and MIDI unchanged.
recSOboeTwo = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { s1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "oboeOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Ob. 1" } } | }
recSTptTwo = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | }
recSTptThree = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | }
recSHnThree = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | }
recSHnFour = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | }
recSCbsn = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vc" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | }
recSTbnOne = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | }
recSTbnTwo = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | }
recSBassTbn = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | }
recSTuba = { R1*26 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | }
% c290 (cyclic; Adler ch. 17, adler2 PDF 273, Ex. XVII-13, Beethoven VC i 99-109: the timpani alone play the motive that joins the two phrases of a soft solo melody):
% 178 the timpani play the scherzo's motive x pp near the rim on G (V) under the muted trumpet's and cor anglais' held D4, the seam between F2's antecedent and consequent;
% the scherzo's last timpani sound (motive x pp-ppp near the rim, 363-371) returns once under the finale's quiet second theme. Same form and pitch as 9 and 143-145 (motiveXretr).
% c326 (playability, timpani; Adler ch. 12, adler2 PDF 80: mallets come in hard, medium and soft, and with the beating spot they set the tone; PDF 61 and 88: the composer names
%   them in words and gives time to change; ch. 14, PDF 110-111): 178's pp motive x near the rim was played with the hard sticks of bar 1 (no stick change between 1 and 215),
%   while it quotes the scherzo's last statements, which its timpanist plays with soft sticks (scherzo coda). 'soft sticks' in the rest at 171 (seven bars to change),
%   'hard sticks' on 178's closing rest for the ff tutti at 199 (twenty bars to change). Notes, dynamics and MIDI unchanged.
recSTimp = { R1^\markup \small \italic "soft sticks" | R1*6 | g,4\pp_\markup \small \italic "near the rim" g,8 g,8 g,4 r4^\markup \small \italic "hard sticks" | R1*18 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | }
